from pathlib import Path
import subprocess
content='''status: done

Review of "'`b716bdf~1..3033e18 -- .claude/skills docs`, covering guard Round 10 and its correction, the four-experts runner, and method 3.8 item 14. Findings below are my reading of the written contracts; reproductions marked observed were run against the reviewed implementation. No implementation, test, configuration, or state file was changed. No other AI engine was invoked; runner checks replaced engine startup with a fake process. Temporary test fixtures were removed.

1. **P1 — The new progress option can abort the runner after launching an expert.** `.claude/skills/four-experts/scripts/four_experts.py:121` (called after startup at lines 179–182). `progress()` opens the requested file without creating its parent. The runner creates seats/briefs/answers/logs, but not `nodes`. **Input:** a fresh run with `--progress runs/<fresh-run>/nodes/experts.progress.md`, with no pre-created nodes directory. **Observed:** mocked startup was called once, then `FileNotFoundError(2, '"'No such file or directory')"'`; no `status.json` was written. With a real process, it has already been launched and this exception provides no cleanup. **Suggested fix:** prepare and validate the progress destination before launching any process; handle later progress-write failures without abandoning process supervision.

2. **P2 — A command that merely mentions deadline start can take another session'"'s deadline.** "'`.claude/skills/workflow-design/guard/src/hooks.ts:759`. The regex searches the entire Bash command, including quoted data, and also runs for PostToolUseFailure. **Input:** session A creates an unclaimed deadline; before its claiming hook, session B completes `echo "deadline start"`; then A'"'s actual start hook runs. **Observed:** B claimed A's deadline and received its clock; A received no clock. T1 specifies the Bash call that actually ran deadline start, and the fails-if forbids reminders reaching another session. **Suggested fix:** recognize an executed invocation of this guard CLI rather than arbitrary command text, exclude failed/nonexecuted starts, and associate the created deadline id with the originating session rather than relying on textual mention. The all-unclaimed claiming policy itself is specified by T1; changing that policy would also require correcting the contract.

3. **P2 — Installation filters out the tools that now need clock reminders.** "'`.claude/skills/workflow-design/guard/src/hooks.ts:813`, `.claude/skills/workflow-design/guard/src/install.ts:29`, `docs/WORKFLOW_DESIGN_METHOD.md:543`. The new wrapper runs only when the harness dispatches the hook. Installed PostToolUse matches `Bash|Write|Edit|MultiEdit`; PostToolUseFailure matches only Bash. **Input:** an active deadline followed by Read, Grep, Glob, or WebSearch calls. **Observed:** generated installation matchers exclude every one of these tools. Reading/research can therefore pass every mark and the deadline without any clock or time-up context. Direct handleHook tests bypass this routing and do not catch it. **Suggested fix:** subscribe the time wrapper to all relevant post-tool events, retain tool-specific filtering inside the older checks, and check delivery through the installation settings. Existing user installations will need the same matcher update.

4. **P2 — The final 100% progress block is recorded as raised but never delivered.** `.claude/skills/workflow-design/guard/src/hooks.ts:766`. The expired branch marks every outstanding percentage as raised and emits only the stop sentence. It never reads or includes workers. **Input:** a 10-minute deadline with a worker progress file, with hooks at minute 9 and exactly minute 10. **Observed:** minute 9 displayed the worker; minute 10 displayed only `harness-guard time is up (0 min over): ...`, although deadline status still returned the worker summary. T5 and method 3.8 item 14 promise the fuller block with progress at 100%. Existing tests at `test/round10-deadline.test.ts:264` check the time-up sentence and journal marker, so they pass while that block is missing. **Suggested fix:** emit the final mark block and up-to-five worker lines once on reaching 100%, then append the time-up sentence on every expired call; record only announcements actually emitted, and assert the final worker block in the test.

5. **P2 — Loaded deadline ids can write outside the deadlines directory.** `.claude/skills/workflow-design/guard/src/deadline.ts:46` and `:75`. The reader accepts any string id; claiming reconstructs the output path from that id rather than the validated input filename. **Input:** `.harness-guard/deadlines/bad.json` containing an otherwise valid unclaimed deadline with `id: "../unexpected"`, followed by a start-claiming hook. **Observed:** `.harness-guard/unexpected.json` was created, while bad.json remained unclaimed. Additional traversal can overwrite an existing JSON file outside the deadline directory if its parent exists. This requires a malformed/manually supplied deadline file; the normal start command generates safe ids. **Suggested fix:** validate ids against `''^D-[0-9a-f]{8}$`, require the filename to match the id, and claim only the file that was read. Validate loaded marks and the computed end time at this boundary too.

6. **P2 — Large finite minutes create a deadline that can never expire.** `.claude/skills/workflow-design/guard/src/deadline.ts:33` and `:81`. Checking that minutes is finite does not check that its millisecond duration or resulting Date is representable. **Input:** `deadline start --minutes 1e308`. **Observed:** exit 0, printed `deadline Inval`; subsequent status exited 0 with `Infinity left, deadline NaN:NaN`. The computed end is Infinity, so up is false for every representable current time and no percentage mark is reached. **Suggested fix:** reject a non-finite duration or an end outside the valid Date range before writing, with usage exit 2; apply the same validation when loading files. This is a representability check, not an invented maximum trial budget.

7. **P2 — Fractional budgets overstate the time overrun at the deadline.** `.claude/skills/workflow-design/guard/src/hooks.ts:770`. The hook subtracts the exact budget from already-rounded usedMinutes. **Input:** start with `minutes: 2.5`, and inject now exactly 150,000 ms after start. **Observed:** the hook says `time is up (0.5 min over)` at the exact deadline; deadlineStatus correctly says `time is up, 0 min over`. Fractional budgets are accepted and used by the tests. **Suggested fix:** subtract the deadline end from now in milliseconds first, then apply the chosen display rounding once, consistently with deadlineStatus.

8. **P2 — Caller-managed experts disappear from the new progress stream.** `.claude/skills/four-experts/scripts/four_experts.py:175` and `:209`. Both caller-handoff branches bypass the added start/finish/progress code; hand_to_caller gives no progress instructions. The skill recommends `--claude-by-caller` inside Claude Code, so this affects its normal use. **Input:** `--only dave --claude-by-caller --progress <existing-parent>/experts.progress.md`. **Observed:** exit 0, no progress file, and Dave'"'s status entry had neither started nor finished. The same omission applies to Fan Li's caller-managed fallback. The script cannot observe external completion without a hand-back mechanism. **Suggested fix:** log the planned/handoff state and give the caller an explicit start/completion recording contract (or provide a completion command); merge those timestamps into status.json when the caller supplies the answer.

9. **P2 — The new mandatory minute-deadline instruction does not cover the accepted round-count limit.** "'`docs/WORKFLOW_DESIGN_METHOD.md:542`, `.claude/skills/workflow-design/SKILL.md:23`. Item 14 accepts a number of rounds and already says to record the last round in state, but the added instructions universally require `deadline start --minutes N`; the new fails-if also rejects a time-boxed run that starts no deadline. **Input:** the owner authorizes three rounds and gives no wall-clock budget. **My reading:** no supplied value can fill N, and the CLI has no round-count mode; obeying the added instruction requires inventing a time allowance or asking again. **Suggested fix:** qualify minute deadlines as applying to wall-clock limits and retain the explicit last-round check for round-limited runs, or add a separately specified round-count monitor. Do not silently convert rounds into minutes.

10. **P2 — The default-wait test accepts a live status after the deadline.** `.claude/skills/workflow-design/guard/test/round10-deadline.test.ts:198`. Its stdout assertion allows either `time is up` or `used ... of 0.05 min`. **Input showing the weak assertion:** a faulty wait implementation sleeps to the correct deadline but prints its cached pre-wait status, e.g. `used 0 of 0.05 min (0%), 1 left, deadline 10:00`. Its exit is 0 and the wall-clock check passes; this regex also passes. This is a test defect, not an observed failure of the current wait implementation. **Suggested fix:** require an expired status for the default 100% wait; test the live-status case separately with an explicit earlier --mark.

Checks and their limits:

- Existing guard suite: `cd .claude/skills/workflow-design/guard && bun test` → **1211 pass, 47 fail**, 1258 tests across 23 files, exit 1. The suite was not authored by this reviewer. Many failures concern empty captured child stdout/stderr.
- Existing Round 10 suite, rerun alone: `bun test test/round10-deadline.test.ts` → **38 pass, 5 fail**, 43 tests, exit 1. All five observed failures are empty CLI stdout assertions. Its file header describes acceptance checks written independently from SPEC; the last every-call check is labelled COO.
- I isolated the capture problem with a trivial `bun -e` parent calling spawnSync on a Bun child that only writes hello and err and exits 0: both captured streams were empty. The same parent'"'s /bin/echo child was captured correctly. Python subprocess captured the real guard CLI output correctly. Therefore I do not attribute those empty-output suite failures to a newly introduced missing CLI print. The cause of this Bun-child capture behavior was not established.
- "'`./node_modules/.bin/tsgo --noEmit -p .` from guard → exit 0, no diagnostics.
- All targeted reproduction checks described as observed above were written and run by this reviewing Codex agent. They establish the reported local behaviors, not independent corroboration or production harness delivery. Engine startup was mocked, so no finding claims a live expert-engine integration test.
- Additional real CLI waits, launched from Python: 50% of 0.02 minutes returned after approximately 0.603 seconds with a live 50% status; 100% returned after approximately 1.204 seconds with time-up. A 24-call concurrent hook check repeated three times produced one mark announcement each time; that sample does not establish freedom from races.

Checked and found nothing — T1 normal default persistence, generated id format, positive/missing-minute validation, mark validation/sorting/deduplication/100 insertion, and ordinary root-relative run conversion.

Checked and found nothing — T2 explicit-session filtering and newest-unexpired/latest-expired selection as SPEC defines it; real CLI local-time display under UTC versus Asia/Kuala_Lumpur.

Checked and found nothing — T3 ordinary default and explicit-50% wait timing in the real CLI checks above. Very long waits and live deadline replacement during a wait were not exercised.

Checked and found nothing — T4 distinct-piece counts, repeated done events, restarted pieces, malformed-line skipping/counting, empty/missing progress files, and exclusion of progress notes/raw text from ordinary summaries.

Checked and found nothing — T5 ordinary sequential 50/80 deduplication, joined existing warning context, five-worker cap, repeated time-up, exact-boundary time-up, and filtering of already-owned other-session deadlines in direct handler calls. Findings 2–4 concern acquisition, installation routing, and the missing final block instead.

Checked and found nothing — T6 once-per-session time asks, joining the criteria reminder, suppressing the ask with an owned deadline, and asking a separate session independently.

Checked and found nothing — four-experts Claude argv correction by source inspection: allowedTools is now one equals-form argument and the prompt remains a distinct argv element. No Claude process was invoked, so parser/integration behavior was not verified. Ordinary runner completion progress and timestamps were inspected; findings 1 and 8 cover the failure and caller-handoff paths.

Checked and found nothing — the node-brief timestamp format matches the progress parser, and method item 14 links to the intended brief and monitor artifacts. Findings 3, 4, and 9 describe the mismatches in its new promises.
